        "thinking": "The TypeScript check is now pulling in all the `packages/db` and server source files, and since we have `\"types\": [\"@cloudflare/workers-types\"]` and no Node types, it's complaining about all the Node.js modules in those packages.\n\nThe right approach here is to use `skipLibCheck: true` (which we get from the base) and also to set `\"types\": [\"@cloudflare/workers-types\", \"node\"]` or include `@types/node` in the types.\n\nBut wait - the whole point is that the CF Worker tsconfig should NOT type-check node:* modules as valid. \n\nThe real problem is that TypeScript is tracing imports from `api.ts` through the server routes into `@paperclipai/db`, which pulls in Node.js modules, and then complains about them. I could add `@types/node` to suppress the errors, but that defeats the purpose of having a CF Worker-specific type check. Alternatively, I could use `skipLibCheck` and exclude workspace packages from type checking, though that might make the check less useful overall.\n\nLooking at the base tsconfig, `skipLibCheck` is already enabled, but the errors are still happening because they're coming from source files in `packages/db/src/` rather than library files. The type checker is traversing the entire import graph and validating everything it finds. The better approach for a monorepo with a CF Worker is probably to use TypeScript's `paths` configuration to point to declaration files instead of source files, which would let me control what gets type-checked.\n\nBut the real problem is that we're importing directly from server source files that depend on Node.js modules, and TypeScript is trying to validate those when it encounters them through the import chain.\n\nThe standard fix for this monorepo setup is to either let Wrangler handle the bundling without running tsc separately, or add both `@cloudflare/workers-types` and `node` to the types array so TypeScript understands both environments. Since we already have `skipLibCheck` in the base config, I could also try using path mappings to redirect workspace imports to their built declaration files instead of source, though those might not exist yet. The quickest solution would be adding the node types to the tsconfig.\n\njson types array and adjusting the include/exclude patterns to avoid tracing through workspace source files we already trust. Project references with composite mode would be cleaner but would require building the server package first. I'll go with the pragmatic approach of updating the tsconfig to include both `@cloudflare/workers-types` and `node` types, since Wrangler will handle the actual bundling and won't include Node modules unless they're imported at runtime.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
